  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Wednesday: The Low has finally reached the \nCalifornia coastline and still pushing upper level clouds across\nthe Pacific Northwest. Dry, warm conditions are expected over \nthe Inland Northwest through most of Wednesday. Precip chances \nacross the region remain near 0 percent through Wednesday. Winds\nwill be generally calm except a brief period of breezy winds \ndown the Okanogan Valley on Tuesday morning with gusts into the \nupper 20 mph range. Omak has a 30-50 percent probability of \ngusts near 30 mph. The midweek highs will be in the 60s and low \n70s. Overnight lows will be in the upper 20s and 30s. Overnight \npatchy frost could impact any remaining gardens and sensitive \nseasonal plants. \n\nLate Wednesday through Friday: The Low finally ejects northeast\ntoward Montana as a trough moves into the Pacific Northwest \novernight Wednesday into Thursday. A wrap around band of \nmoisture will bring increased shower chances to Southeast WA and\nthe Idaho Panhandle on Thursday and Friday with a 40-80 percent\nprobability of at least a tenth of an inch. A trough pattern \nwill be over the Pacific Northwest behind the exiting Low. \nFriday will have mountain showers with breezy winds across the \nBasin. Wind speeds will be teens with gust into the 25-35 mph \nrange for most. Waterville Plateau and Southeast WA have a 20-40\npercent probability of reaching 40 mph gusts. Temperatures will\ntrend cooler. Thursday and Friday highs will be in 50s to low \n60s. Overnight lows will be in the 30s.\n\nSaturday through Monday: Through the weekend, models are \nstruggling on the incoming low and precip chances. The 3-7 day \ncluster analysis shows a near 50/50 split on whether the Inland \nnorthwest will be under a flat ridge or widespread precip Low. \nThe consensus is keeping the precip on the west side of the \nCascades. Current forecast keeps the small chance of showers \nacross the Inland Northwest. If the drying trend continues, the \nprecip chances will likely start to decline. Snow levels \ndropping down to around 4000 feet through the weekend. Back \ncountry trekkers should prepare for cold, possibly light snow \nconditions. Highs will be in the 50s to low 60s. Overnight lows \nwill be in the upper 20s and 30s. /JDC",
